1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,950
Lesson 11: four-square and other receiving antennas

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:25,342
YA-NEC course, lesson 11: the Four-square and other receiving antennas. First the Four-square, in Calculators.

3
00:00:25,392 --> 00:00:34,603
Four lambda/4 verticals for 7.1 MHz on the corners of a 10.56 m square, 4 raised radials each, as in ON4UN.

4
00:00:34,653 --> 00:00:51,231
Fed with current sources: 0, -90, -90 and -180 degrees. 4.1 dBi over average ground, F/B 19.4 dB, beamwidth 100 degrees; the buttons 0-90-180-270 switch direction.

5
00:00:51,281 --> 00:01:00,765
The table shows the resulting impedances: 22 to 56 ohm, all different. That is why a four-square needs a proper phasing network.

6
00:01:00,815 --> 00:01:08,926
Now the Receiving antennas family of the Calculators: the Flag (K6SE, W7IUV).

7
00:01:08,976 --> 00:01:56,459
A terminated rectangle 8.8 x 4.28 m, 937 ohm resistor, 16:1 transformer.

8
00:01:56,509 --> 00:02:09,604
RDF 7.8 dB, just like the IV3PRK measurement (7.8-8.0); F/B 38.5 dB, gain -30 dBi: 20 dB of preamplifier.

9
00:02:09,654 --> 00:02:39,338
'Optimize for F/B'.

10
00:02:39,388 --> 00:02:46,700
950 ohm: F/B 39.2 dB. The Flag is already close to its best.

11
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:56,514
Waller Flag (NX4D, N4IS): two Flags 7.73 m apart, phased by lines - IV3PRK's 2007 model.

12
00:02:56,564 --> 00:03:52,990
600 ohm loads, 100 ohm lines, the rear one crossed: the second Flag cancels what the first one still hears from behind.

13
00:03:53,040 --> 00:04:02,338
RDF 11.6 dB (IV3PRK: 11.84) - better than a 175 m Beverage - in a 12 m garden.

14
00:04:02,388 --> 00:04:08,974
The price is the signal: -58.5 dBi. Two preamplifiers are needed.

15
00:04:09,024 --> 00:04:14,708
The ranking with the antennas computed in this lesson and the last one.

16
00:04:14,758 --> 00:04:24,775
RDF from 5 dB of the vertical to over 13 dB of the long Beverages and Hi-Z arrays: choose by space, money and how much you want to hear.

17
00:04:24,825 --> 00:04:36,763
Four-square, Flag, Waller Flag: receiving is a matter of RDF, not of gain. Next lesson: loaded loops, toroids and the model objects. 73 de IC8TEM
